  "comments": [],
  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "About 170 Viking swords carry the same inlaid name. Ulfberht, spelled out in iron letters down the middle of the blade.",
        "visual_query": "viking age iron sword blade",
        "visual_fallback": "sword blade"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They turn up across Scandinavia and the Baltic, forty-four in Norway alone, made between the ninth and eleventh centuries.",
        "visual_query": "norwegian fjord viking landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "cold fjord"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Seventeen of them spell it wrong. Was somebody copying a name they could not read, a thousand years before trademarks?",
        "visual_query": "medieval blacksmith forge anvil",
        "visual_fallback": "forge sparks"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The name is Frankish, so the workshop was probably in the Rhineland. Three of the swords turned up in Volga Bulgaria, far down the Russian river trade route.",
        "visual_query": "rhine river valley germany",
        "visual_fallback": "river valley"
      },
      {
        "narration": "And the best blades are not local metal at all: one is high-carbon steel that may have come from Central Asia. A Frankish brand, foreign steel, seventeen bad spellers.",
        "visual_query": "volga river wide steppe",
        "visual_fallback": "wide river"
      }
    ],
